WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.800
- But before all that, we need to talk

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.220
to a guy we are obsessed with.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.340
I personally am.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.040
I love him. I've known him for years.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.110
I love doing his show,
especially the day drinking part.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.180
He's the host of Late Night on NBC

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.847
and his new kids book is called

00:00:12.847 --> 00:00:14.430
"I'm Not Scared, YOU'RE Scared!"

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.263
and it's out now.

00:00:15.263 --> 00:00:16.096
I love that title.

00:00:16.096 --> 00:00:17.090
He is on the line right now.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:17.923
What's up Seth?

00:00:17.923 --> 00:00:18.756
(audience cheers, lively music plays)

00:00:18.756 --> 00:00:19.589
- Kelly!

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:28.440
- Hello! So first I'm very
excited to talk about this,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.210
but first happy St. Patrick's Day.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.210
So what's like your best New
York St. Patty's Day memory?

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.070
- Well, my best St. Patty's
day memories are Chicago based.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.650
That's where I went to
college and we used to go

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.560
to downtown Chicago and drink green beer

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.270
in plastic solo cups.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.530
And green beer is great
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.480
because it basically is telling you

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.660
how your night's gonna end
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.470
because the green beer
doesn't end up in the belly

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.609
the green beer sort of it's
a gift back to the world.

00:00:57.609 --> 00:00:59.310
(audience laughs)
- It makes for an encore.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.150
It has an encore.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.440
It comes back. (chuckles)

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.090
- Yeah, so basically when you drink it,

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.347
it says, "I'll see you soon."

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:07.042
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:01:07.042 --> 00:01:09.390
- (laughing) I don't know
if I've ever had green beer.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.290
It doesn't sound enticing.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.270
So your new book is called "I'm
Not Scared, YOU'RE Scared!"

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:15.330
Can you tell us about it?

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.670
And I love the title by the way. (laughs)

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:18.503
- Thank you.

00:01:18.503 --> 00:01:20.399
I was gonna write one based
on our day drinking called

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:22.180
"I'm Not Drunk, You're Drunk"
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.890
but the publisher said
that wasn't good for kids.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.943
- It would also be a great book. (laughs)

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:28.810
- I wanted to write a book.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.400
I'm so fascinated, having kids,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.470
with how they approach
their own fears and anxiety.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.730
So I wanted to write a story

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.900
that had a beginning, middle, and end

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.000
and some excitement and
a bit of an adventure

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.730
but also was maybe the kind of book

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.530
where parents and kids could talk

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.380
about things that their kids are afraid of

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.230
and how sometimes kids
are afraid for no reason,

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.400
and sometimes they're smart to be afraid.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.170
And as a parent, you appreciate
when they don't wanna go

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.420
to the tallest part of the jungle gym

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.900
in a playground when
they're not old enough.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.270
So it's hopefully a nice
discussion about fear and anxiety.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.070
That also is fun for
parents to read to kids.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.150
- Yeah. I mean, honestly,
as a parent, I mean,

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:08.140
I couldn't think of a better book.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.230
I read every night to my kids,

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.640
and there's been so much fear
and anxiety just around COVID

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.230
about everything that's been
happening the last two years.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.090
And then there's just so much
fear and anxiety for everyone

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:19.170
that do you ever, your kids are young

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.870
but do you ever, does any
of these topics ever come up

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.750
like how to deal with that?

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.070
- You know, they're a little bit young,

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.590
but certainly the fact that
they've been wearing masks

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.590
for a couple of years,

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.520
it's impossible not to talk to them

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.890
about what we're going through

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.060
and how sort of a collective sacrifice

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.660
is necessary for the
health of and wellbeing of

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.340
of those who are, you know,

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.760
more compromised during a
difficult time like this.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.260
So you try to have those conversations

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.340
in a way they understand
while appreciating

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.490
they're probably a couple
years away from fully

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.380
being able to grasp everything
that's going on in the world.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.240
And that isn't necessarily a bad thing.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.930
I wanna let them be
kids as much as possible

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.150
and they can deal with adult
life when that rolls around.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.360
- Absolutely, it's funny though.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.580
My kids, like, pre-COVID...
I'm the parent,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.007
I've never been the parent that's like

00:03:07.007 --> 00:03:08.820
"oh my God, don't touch
her," or "don't lick that,"

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.907
you know, I'm like the
parent to my kid that's like

00:03:11.907 --> 00:03:14.060
"oh, it's free probiotics, it's fine.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.520
It dropped on the ground, like
just, you're gonna be fine."

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.800
So I went from that person,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.190
now my kids are like masked up

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:20.850
and we're going out and
I'm like, "don't touch it."

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.776
And they're like "you
said, free probiotics."

00:03:22.776 --> 00:03:23.640
(audience laughs)

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.660
Like they literally
threw "free probiotic."

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.310
My daughter threw it back in my face.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.970
And it was an interesting
conversation how to explain that.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:32.320
But I love this,

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.770
and you wrote that you
are hoping your children,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.130
I love this, hoping your
children like this book

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.900
because none of them can stay up

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.796
late enough to watch your show. (laughs)

00:03:40.796 --> 00:03:41.629
(audience laughs)

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:42.462
- Right.

00:03:42.462 --> 00:03:43.295
- So that's amazing.

00:03:43.295 --> 00:03:45.890
So they have no idea who you are really.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.250
And it's like, it's amazing,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.840
cause you have such an
impact on pop culture

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.000
and they have no idea.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.320
And it's awesome that this
will be their introduction.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.570
Do they like the book?

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.150
- They do like the book.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.950
I told them the story first

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:01.220
before I put it down on
paper and got their approval.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.870
Their favorite part of the book

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.300
by far is the dedication page

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.850
because I dedicated it to them.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:09.100
And so they show that to people

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.048
as if it means they wrote the book.

00:04:11.048 --> 00:04:13.950
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.580
- Well, thank you so much, Seth.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.320
I love you so much.
(audience applauds)

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:18.520
I love the book!

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.720
I will be reading this
with my kids tonight.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.087
Everybody pick up his new book,

00:04:24.087 --> 00:04:27.153
"I'm Not Scared, YOU'RE
Scared!" it's out right now.

